filiation":"Department of Chemistry, University of Houston, Houston, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Dedek K","lastName":"Dedek","firstName":"Karin","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1490-0141"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Animal Navigation/Neurosensorics, Institute for Biology and Environmental Sciences, University of Oldenburg, Oldenburg, Germany; Research Center Neurosensory Science, University of Oldenburg, Oldenburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Zoidl G","lastName":"Zoidl","firstName":"Georg","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology, University of Osnabrück, Osnabrück, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Hensel M","lastName":"Hensel","firstName":"Michael","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology/Chemistry, iBiOs-Integrated Bioimaging Facility, CellNanOs - Center of Cellular Nanoanalytics, Osnabrück, Germany; Department of Microbiology, University of Osnabrück, Osnabrück, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"O'Brien J","lastName":"O'Brien","firstName":"John","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0270-3442"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Optometry, University of Houston, Houston, Texas, USA. Electronic address: CONTACT_EMAIL."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5579-2437"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8916-6311"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9880-5939"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0270-3442"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3140-1523"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9025-0446"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1490-0141"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3016-2978"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"299","journalIssueId":3668442,"dateOfPublication":"2023 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2023,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","title":"The Journal of biological chemistry","essn":"1083-351X","issn":"0021-9258","isoabbreviation":"J Biol Chem","nlmid":"2985121R"},"printPublicationDate":"2023-11-01"},"pubYear":"2023","pageInfo":"105282","abstractText":"The intracellular domains of connexins are essential for the assembly of gap junctions. For connexin 36 (Cx36), the major neuronal connexin, it has been shown that a dysfunctional PDZ-binding motif interferes with electrical synapse formation. However, it is still unknown how this motif coordinates the transport of Cx36. In the present study, we characterize a phenotype of Cx36 mutants that lack a functional PDZ-binding motif using HEK293T cells as an expression system. We provide evidence that an intact PDZ-binding motif is critical for proper endoplasmic reticulum (ER) export of Cx36. Removing the PDZ-binding motif of Cx36 results in ER retention and the formation of multimembrane vesicles containing gap junction-like connexin aggregates. Using a combination of site-directed mutagenesis and electron micrographs, we reveal that these vesicles consist of Cx36 channels that docked prematurely in the ER. Our data suggest a model in which ER-retained Cx36 channels reshape the ER membrane into concentric whorls that are released into the cytoplasm.","affiliation":"College of Optometry, University of Houston, Houston, Texas, USA. Electronic address: CONTACT_EMAIL.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NEI NIH HHS","acronym":"EY","grantId":"F31 EY034793","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"R01EY012857","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NEI NIH HHS","acronym":"EY","grantId":"P30 EY007551","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft",